{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D131-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"9423305cf13920f90083a319e290351f364fdc6c828e9795309c9feb0c509565\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 offers a menu secret, but A3 and A5 never disclose increasingly sensitive details.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 tests B2's promise; A5 treats B4's naming of Sam as a confidentiality risk.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 promises discretion and B4 identifies Sam, but B has little personal stake.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 announces a lock to check without increasing the sensitivity of A1's disclosure.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes club news, although A5's lock metaphor obscures the next playable step.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 is economical; A5 supplies a polished metaphor instead of another trust test.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 56\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The club's dropping Tuesday dinners. Can that stay between us?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yes. Sam will ask why I'm eating at home, though.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Tell Sam you're saving money. The club can't pay the chef. Keep that separate.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can. I'm the treasurer; I already know.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then here's what isn't in your ledger: I've been paying him myself. Even Sam can't know that.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A tests discretion by releasing progressively more sensitive information before granting deeper trust.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary secrecy withholds a fact; this card requires successive disclosures that test the recipient.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 and A5 only discuss confidentiality; revised A1, A3 and A5 progress from cancelled dinners to unpaid wages to personal funding.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a tests a club treasurer whose knowledge changes the disclosure; alternative b tests a sibling with divided family loyalties.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original never performs the required disclosure ladder. The revision adds a club funding situation. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2's concern produces A3's cover explanation; B4's existing knowledge prompts A5's more private funding admission. Five spoken ABABA turns and 71 words pass the structural gates.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 makes one further disclosure without waiting for a fresh promise; performance must convey that B4's responsible handling of existing knowledge earns it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D131-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"28b66ad3de38f992cb9fb6725d41ef7fbbf403858970cd389f178cd4cf920df3\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies one secret; A5 merely promises a reason rather than sharing something more sensitive.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2's why prompts A3's confidentiality question, and B4's assurance licenses A5's conditional offer.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 shows curiosity, but B4's unqualified nowhere gives A no meaningful relational complication.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 stops before the deeper disclosure that would develop A1's trust test.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 names an application but leaves its purpose and B's connection unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's sentence going somewhere is concise, but A5 remains abstract and anticipatory.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 56\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I withdrew my college application. Don't tell Mum yet.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm your sister, not her newsletter. Why?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've rented a bakery. Can you keep an address secret too?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yes, but she'll notice you leaving every morning.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You can say I'm working. Don't say I bought her old bakery. That's the part I'm trusting only you with.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A uses successively more consequential disclosures to test whether B merits a more intimate confidence.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simply delaying an explanation can resemble this card without actually testing trust through increasing sensitivity.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 postpones the reason; revised A3 reveals the bakery and A5 reveals its private connection to their mother.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b uses sibling reassurance and a practical cover story; alternative a discovers that a treasurer already knows the intermediate secret.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original ends before its central behavior develops. The revision introduces a family and bakery setting. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 4, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 89. B2's sibling assurance earns A3's business disclosure; B4's concern about visible absences makes A5 distinguish permissible information from the family secret. Five spoken ABABA turns remain below 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"Rented versus bought could imply different arrangements for premises and business, but that distinction is unnecessary ambiguity; the family significance remains the intended escalation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D132-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"3a7c6b449a9bb6be31fd87074351aa21214c978457c1e9b0fe6b4a4e9b3ef804\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 demands the actual note; A5 bargains for the decisive line so A can inspect the evidence.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's reading offer with an emphasis objection; A5 narrows access in response to B4's privacy concern.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers mediated access and B4 asserts privacy, forcing A5 to negotiate a smaller surrender.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 reveals that B is using the note to decide for A, sharpening the stakes of A1's request.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a tangible note, but only A5 establishes why inspection matters and never names the decision.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's words without B's emphasis expresses the access dispute economically; A5 avoids an unrelated joke.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 88\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Give me the actual note, not your description.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can read it to you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I want the words that don't get your emphasis.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's private.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then show me the one line you're using to decide for me.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A negotiates the surrender of inspectable evidence that would let A participate in a decision.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A general demand for honesty need not seek an object or access that enables a concrete next step.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 seeks the actual note, A3 rejects mediated wording, and A5 proposes limited direct access.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a bargains around privacy by reducing the request; alternative b reframes documentary access as shared responsibility.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original passes at 88 and needs no rewrite. A fresh internal reread preserves all six ratings: B2 causes A3's emphasis objection, while B4 causes A5's narrower access proposal. The five spoken turns meet the gates and remain under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The unnamed decision makes the opening less immediately situated, although A5 supplies enough personal stakes to play the negotiation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D132-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"cb04515247b17e51dd5527fcdb0a63cb87cb899bf8b98b3e461e23eb2ca0b666\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 requests the drawer key to check figures; A3 explains why B's summary cannot enable that work.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 distinguishes calculation from B2's total; A5 answers B4's trust challenge with shared responsibility.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers a substitute and B4 personalizes the request, requiring A5 to protect the relationship.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns A1's procedural request into an offer to move responsibility onto the records.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 gives actors a locked drawer and figures to verify, though their institutional roles remain open.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5's paperwork sharing responsibility compactly answers B4's emotional objection and continues the persuasion.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Hand me the drawer key so I can check the figures.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll tell you the total.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'm checking how the total was built.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Don't you trust me?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'd like the paperwork to share the responsibility.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A persuades a gatekeeper to release access to records needed for independent verification.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Expressing distrust alone would not fulfill the drive unless it served the effort to obtain enabling access.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 specifies both the key and its purpose; A3 and A5 supply successive reasons for surrendering it.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b softens a trust challenge through shared accountability; alternative a limits inspection to accommodate privacy.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original passes at 92. A fresh internal reread confirms the same ratings and intact causal links: B2 prompts A3's distinction between total and calculation, and B4 prompts A5's relational reassurance. Exact dialogue retained; all structural gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 implies the key request rather than repeating it, so its persuasive purpose depends on maintaining attention on the locked records.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D133-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"4a49beff1df8a1638d89fb866ba518329f69911fcf291866a00db0cecc84d2d7\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 redirects A's signature toward the driver; A5 redirects the matching ink toward the pen supplier.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's signed delivery becomes A3's driver contact, and B4's pen clue becomes A5's supplier investigation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 pursue evidence against A, repeatedly forcing A to manufacture a safer investigative direction.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands suspicion from the delivery driver to the pen supplier as B4 brings the evidence closer to A.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a stain and delivery packaging; B4 introduces a readily playable matching pen.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's wider investigation follows the exact ink clue rather than adding a detached punchline.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That stain could be from the delivery packaging. Let's check the van.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You signed for the delivery.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. I know which driver to ask.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's the same ink as your pen.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then the pen supplier may be involved. Wider investigation.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A converts each clue pointing toward A into a reason to investigate a safer external suspect.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D134 avoids the dangerous subject; D133 stays with each clue and assigns it a less incriminating explanation.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 uses the delivery signature to implicate the driver, and A5 uses the ink match to implicate the supplier.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a widens a mock investigation through successive suppliers; alternative b turns a partner's corrective evidence into a joint warranty claim.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original passes at 90. A fresh internal reread confirms those ratings: each accusation provides the material for A's next redirection, and B4 specifically causes the supplier theory in A5. Exact five-turn dialogue retained.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A's actual responsibility is implied by the evasions and matching ink rather than explicitly admitted, appropriately leaving the actor to hold the concealment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D133-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e65fba0842500c07043dace8c88ed967f3ee4f140e6d301f60cf5ec12f1db314\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 blames the bracket and A3 the instructions, but A5's paper complaint barely explains A's disposal of them.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 plausibly responds to B2's upside-down fitting; A5 does not establish how paper quality caused B4's discarded instructions.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 know A's actions and actively dismantle each proposed external explanation.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds another supplier complaint but offers a weaker explanation as the evidence tightens.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's failed shelf and B2's inverted bracket establish a concrete domestic repair problem.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 is concise; A5's supplier callback has rhythm but outruns its causal explanation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 73\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your shelf fell because they supplied the wrong bracket.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You fitted it upside down. Look at the arrow.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That's a misleading arrow. We should photograph it for the manufacturer.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You drew that arrow. I watched you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Because theirs was missing. Put that in the complaint; they made us supply our own safety markings.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A conceals faulty workmanship by converting each increasingly personal clue into evidence of manufacturer failure.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic blame shifting can repeat an accusation without adapting to the new clue that threatens exposure.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 explains away the arrow; after B4 identifies A as its author, A5 recasts drawing it as compensation for missing markings.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b recruits the aggrieved shelf owner into a complaint; alternative a keeps moving the investigation toward different outside suspects.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original B4–A5 link needs a causal explanation. The revision retains the shelf setting and introduces an arrow as concrete evidence. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 95. B4's eyewitness correction causes A5 to reinterpret A's own marking as the supplier's omission. Five spoken turns remain below 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The manufacturer explanation is transparently opportunistic; A must sincerely pursue the complaint rather than acknowledge the excuse as a joke.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D134-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c2d6e8a20ada253808d81f5963f2f62313ee687269483213112d06286bb41b53\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A3 steer away from possessing the key, though B4 is about transport rather than another attempt to discuss it.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 refuses B2's repeated question; A5 responds directly to B4's denial that they are taking a bus.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants an explanation, but B4 joins the transport topic and weakens pressure on the key.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 preserves the distraction without increasing the consequence of avoiding the key question.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 names a key and timetable but supplies no natural reason the key's possession is exposing.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns B4's correction into another transport problem with little padding.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 71\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll return your spare key after dinner. Have you booked somewhere?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I never gave you a spare key. Where did you get it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Let's get a table before everywhere fills up.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've booked. Did my sister give you that key?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"For two or three? We can't leave your sister without dinner.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A repeatedly diverts discussion from the key's source, the single detail that could expose A's involvement.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D133 would explain the key through a safer culprit; this drive redirects attention away from explaining it.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 substitutes restaurant availability for B2's source question; A5 turns B4's named sister into a dinner guest.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses practical hosting concerns to evade a key's source; alternative b uses affectionate anniversary planning to evade a companion's identity.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original lets B4 abandon the dangerous detail. The revision adds an unauthorized spare key and dinner booking. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4 removes the table excuse and identifies a possible source; A5 uses both facts to create a guest-count distraction. Five spoken turns remain below 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 risks sounding like an inadvertent admission about the sister, which creates useful exposure pressure but should not settle the mystery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D134-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ff23b379e12b6e9ba1ba8d38b6787aa7b74029d551732647981293301c2e0004\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A5 steer away from identity, while A3 partly argues identification is difficult rather than redirecting.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4's coat can motivate A5's colour remark, but A1 places the person outside the frame despite B2 and B4 seeing identifying details.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 investigate the person, but the relationship and reason this discovery matters remain unspecified.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds photographer talk without making the identity evasion substantially more consequential.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1's person outside the frame conflicts with the visible coat in B4, leaving the photographic evidence unclear.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's colour combination follows the coat but the photographer question feels like a second generic escape.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 63\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"This holiday photo would make a lovely anniversary card. Look at that sunset.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Who's holding your hand at the edge?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Would you prefer the beach photo? More room for a message.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Same person, same bracelet. Who is it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That bracelet's exactly your colour. Shall I find you one for our anniversary?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A turns each attempt to identify a compromising companion into a discussion of an anniversary gift.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Inventing an innocent identity would steer suspicion; A instead avoids providing any identity at all.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 changes photographs rather than answering B2, and A5 uses B4's bracelet clue to propose a present.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b shifts from card design to a personal gift under intimate scrutiny; alternative a continually repurposes dinner logistics.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original has unclear visual geography and limited stakes. The revision introduces anniversary planning and a visibly cropped companion. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's repeated bracelet evidence directly supplies A5's gift diversion. Five spoken turns remain below 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The handholding strongly suggests romantic concealment, although the dialogue leaves the actual identity and motive unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D135-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"2bbac57eae25fe97081b8d74a0c47b138bb145168f11cbb8d0c32485b4e7c02a\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 edits the rejection to its practical decision; A3 explicitly withholds hurtful adjectives until B has eaten.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's request for omitted content, and A5 counters B4's demand to read with the established breakfast concern.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 assert a right to the complete letter while A3 and A5 pursue protective control.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 develops selective reporting into control over when B may confront the complete rejection.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a gallery rejection; A3 and A5 place the protective exchange at breakfast over toast.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's cold toast argument grows from A3's breakfast concern and A1's unchanged gallery decision.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The gallery can't use the painting. That's the useful part of the letter.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"What else does it say?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Several adjectives you don't need before breakfast.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Let me read them.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Eat first. The decision won't change with cold toast.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A protects the rejected painter by reducing a hurtful letter to its practical outcome and managing access to the rest.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Avoiding one's own difficult announcement would protect A; this scene's editing is directed at sparing B's feelings.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 reports the rejection honestly while A3 identifies the omitted adjectives as unsuitable for B before breakfast.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a controls exposure to a written rejection through domestic care; alternative b supplies a face-saving account and a replacement social plan.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original passes at 92. A fresh internal reread confirms the same ratings, with B4's demand causing A5's breakfast-based postponement. The exact five-turn dialogue stays under 75 words and leaves further negotiation possible.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 shifts from edited explanation toward delayed access, but A1 and A3 already establish the precise protective editing required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D135-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"29190c03834456e3c8d96135d83637c4622b3d0fcedd988e2efb441571b98d3c\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 softens exclusion as a changed guest list, but A3 and A5 describe editing instead of offering more of the kinder explanation.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 avoids B2's unkindness question, and A5 acknowledges B4's charge of incompleteness.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 seek candor, but A3's better evening never becomes a concrete offer responsive to B.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 restates A3's forward-looking justification without increasing commitment or changing the protective tactic.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a guest-list change, while A3 and A5 leave the supposed alternative evening unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's somewhere to go next is polished but abstract rather than a playable next destination.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 67\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Nina's keeping her birthday dinner small. You and I can get noodles.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Small? She invited the whole choir.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"She's choosing people she sees every week. It isn't a verdict on your friendship.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We rehearse together every Tuesday. Read me her message.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"She wants an uncomplicated evening. That's the kindest accurate sentence. Come have one with me.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A edits a social rejection into the least hurtful accurate account and offers companionship to protect B.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Mere cheerful distraction would not supply the carefully softened explanation that this card requires.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 calls the dinner small, A3 frames attendance as familiarity, and A5 selects the kindest accurate sentence from the message.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b commits A to sharing a replacement evening; alternative a tries to preserve an ordinary breakfast before the full written criticism arrives.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original ends in abstract commentary about kindness. The revision adds a choir birthday exclusion and a noodles invitation. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4 defeats A3's explanation and requests the source, causing A5 to offer a narrower edited quotation plus personal companionship. Five spoken turns remain below 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A1's small-dinner account may shade into false reassurance; B2's correction should make A's subsequent careful editing feel protective rather than casually deceptive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D136-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"351625cc8c0a97c4b34a40750112c3f27bb7f90a57420e9e4e59b96d2ade4d75\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 offers a matching confession as bait, but A3 largely repeats the fear already disclosed in A1 and A5 stops baiting.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 turns B2's fear of anger toward underlying fear; A5 recognizes B4's fear of being displaced.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 reveal vulnerability about A's anger and loyalty, giving the withheld invitation personal stakes.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 comments on achieving depth instead of offering a new truth to pursue B4's revealing admission.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a hidden invitation and a directly comparable past act; A5 recalls the envelope.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's more than an envelope summarizes the scene's meaning instead of continuing its interpersonal action.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 72\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I once hid an invitation because I felt left out. Why hide mine?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You'd have gone without me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I nearly skipped your birthday when you invited Leah. Is this about being alone?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's about you liking her more.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I counted your photos with her after that party. Did you hide mine to make me choose you?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reveals increasingly personal pieces of A's own insecurity to draw out the motive behind B's concealment.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Empathetic reassurance may share feelings without using those disclosures to elicit the reason for a particular choice.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Each revised A turn couples a personal admission with a question about B's hidden invitation; A5 uses photo-counting to test B's desire to be chosen.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses reciprocal jealousy within a close friendship; alternative b offers embarrassing workplace history to investigate a travel decision.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original A5 closes the inquiry with commentary. The revision keeps the hidden invitation and adds a share